{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeDxvjddt1GKKj3D2JTTz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019W1tT1N5pY9taqKEmkz3rQ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat > evidence/decode_loop/COMMIT_MSG_1p4.txt <<'MSG'\n1.4: the ~49k dynamic-shared-memory ceiling is closed, gated through the engine's own decode\nstep, and it is a measured NULL on throughput -- proven by a same-arm control, not by argument\n\nWHAT WAS BROKEN. Six top-k launches and `sdpa` sized their dynamic shared memory from a runtime\nquantity (~4T bytes; (head_dim+seq)*4). Past this device's 49,152 B per-block default the LAUNCH\nfails, the following `cudaDeviceSynchronize` returns SUCCESS, and the consumer proceeds on a buffer\nnothing wrote. Iteration 5 landed the opt-in for the four scan kernels but its server build was\nkilled mid-nvcc, so it was never compiled into the engine and the item was never closed.\n\nWHAT WAS MEASURED THIS ITERATION.\n\n1. The full enumeration. Every `<<<g,b,smem,s>>>` in kernels/ and src/: 13 request dynamic shared\n   memory, 7 scale with a runtime quantity (6 top-k + `sdpa`), 6 are bounded by model or block\n   constants at <= 4 KiB. `sdpa` is the one iteration 5 missed -- and it is on the one path nothing\n   links, which is why it would have sat there. Fixed and gated (tests/gate_sdpa_smem.cu).\n\n2. The leg above the ceiling, through the ENGINE, which is what the ladder entry asked for.\n   tests/gate_topk_smem_ctx.cu drives `compressed_decode_step_indexer` -- the function the server\n   calls once per decode step -- at contexts 8,191 / 49,207 / 200,003 / 240,003. Radix, fallback\n   scan and in-situ reference arms all agree bit-exactly above the ceiling; past the opt-in maximum\n   it aborts with the numbers. It could NOT be run in build/dsv4-server: seqmax 49,152 needs\n   6.15 GiB of seqmax-scaling allocations against 2.05 GiB at today's 16,384 and 3.7 GiB free.\n\n3. The defect reproduced in the SAME binary. New DSV4_TOPK_SMEM_OPTIN=0 restores pre-1.4 behaviour:\n   launch fails `invalid argument`, sync says success, exit code 0, output 4096/4096 nonzero with\n   0 NaN -- and the wrong hash. This CORRECTS iteration 5's own write-up twice: it is not zeros\n   (`dtop` is unzeroed `dmalloc`, so it is a plausible wrong answer), and the in-situ reference does\n   not \"agree with anything\" -- it reports a FALSE FAIL against the correct shipped path.\n\n4. A hardware fact and a margin that failed a passing gate. `MaxSharedMemoryPerBlockOptin` is not a\n   kernel's settable maximum; subtract the kernel's own STATIC shared, and do NOT subtract\n   `ReservedSharedMemoryPerBlock`. Bisected: static 0 -> 232,448 settable; static 1,024 -> 231,424.\n   Subtracting `reserved` \"to be safe\" aborted gate_topk_radix's T=58,045 leg minutes after it\n   passed.\n\n5. THE RATCHET, and the control is the finding. First 1.4 leg: 19.62 tok/s against 1.3's recorded\n   20.89 -- -6.1 %, outside the quoted 3.5 % spread -- with tau and token ids identical, on a change\n   whose three touched TUs compile to BYTE-IDENTICAL SASS. So the pre-1.4 binary was rebuilt from\n   e4d7c6d and run now:\n\n     tau           2.87 recorded | 2.87/2.87/2.87 pre-1.4 today | 2.87/2.87/2.87 with 1.4\n     spec tok/s   20.89 recorded | 19.65-19.72   pre-1.4 today | 19.65-19.76   with 1.4\n     base AR      11.43 recorded | 11.28         pre-1.4 today | 11.37         with 1.4\n     token ids -- byte-identical across all three (md5 25c90e85...)\n\n   The old binary does not reproduce its own number. Three replicates inside ONE checkpoint load\n   spread 0.6 %; the same measurement across loads three hours apart differs by 5.7 %. The dominant\n   variance on this box is BETWEEN loads. Corollary, now a rule: a number from a previous iteration\n   is not a valid before-arm.\n\nWHAT IT MEANS. 1.4 moves no throughput number and could not have. What it buys is that the two arms\nwhich certify every future top-k change -- DSV4_TOPK_RADIX=0 and DSV4_TOPK_GATE=1 -- now work above\nthe context they were only ever run below, instead of condemning the code they audit. Next item is\n1.5 (`i:score`), and it is a kernel change: this is the second consecutive iteration without one.\n\nAlso closed in passing, because CLAUDE.md requires it: detach_audit.sh knew neither `run_model.sh`\nnor `build/decode`, so an audit taken while this iteration's own benchmark was running printed one\nrow for a three-process tree and said \"all detached\".\n\nWiki: kernel-optimisations.md (1.4 closed), measurement-and-traps.md (SS17 rewritten from the\nengine-path measurement, SS18 the opt-in maximum, SS19 between-load variance),\ncontext-ceiling-is-not-the-kv-cache.md (the third launch; the current 134,276 B/token footprint),\nREADME.md state table.\nMSG\nwc -l evidence/decode_loop/COMMIT_MSG_1p4.txt","description":"Write the prepared commit message"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1110,"cache_read_input_tokens":267189,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1110},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ca49309a-d7ef-4fa0-bb9f-d3d21ec00295","uuid":"d7b5dc50-f333-4f49-836d-8956e4ea8151","timestamp":"2026-08-20T12:01:56.406Z","request_id":"req_011CeDxvgpxosgoxyQvZ9svN"}
