{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019Zm2oUwC1gporD5AxxDpHR","type":"tool_result","content":"116:## 11. Gate the shape range the engine spans\n125:## 12. The engine stops reproducing itself part-way through a long run, so \"bit-identical token ids\" is not a test there\n179:## 13. A gate that passed, reported as a failure, because the probe it rode on kept no records\n200:## 14. A phase that ran nothing, and reported \"finished\"\n239:## 15. Put a leg the change *cannot* touch inside the same sweep\n278:## 16. A fitted slope is only \"cost per 1000 context\" if the mark is linear in context\n313:## 17. A launch that failed, and a `cudaStreamSynchronize` that called it success\n393:## 18. The device opt-in maximum is not the kernel's maximum, and the safety margin that failed a passing gate\n428:## 19. The variance is BETWEEN checkpoint loads, not within them — 0.6 % vs 5.7 %\n471:## 20. A gate that does not compile is a gate that passes by never running (ladder 1.5, 2026-08-20)\n519:## 21. dprof reads a saving 15–20 % low, so it attributes but does not ratchet (ladder 1.5)\n542:## 22. `tau` reproduced to four decimals; `tok/s` on the same protocol did not — and the promotion gate keys on the second one (ladder 2.2, 2026-08-20)\n564:### 22.1 The consequence for the selection rule\n588:### 22.2 The 17 % that is not in the answer but should be in someone's notebook\n606:## 23. A drift control that failed — and the agent that was contaminating its own arms (ladder 3.1, 2026-08-20)\n655:## 24. The base-AR window times the governor, not the engine — and it is 21 % (ladder 3.1, 2026-08-20)\n692:## 25. A unit gate that stops six times short of the regime it is quoted about (ladder 1.9, 2026-08-20)\n721:## 26. A field that was never computed compares equal, and equal reads as exonerated (ladder 1.9)\n749:## 27. `build/decode` and `dsv4-server` do not share a prefill, and a bit-exactness result on one says nothing about the other (ladder 1.9)\n770:## 28. A tuning default that was a regression at its own design point, and the mechanism story that was wrong (ladder 1.7, 2026-08-20)\n==== tail ====\nbinary that runs in seconds, so re-running it is free — which is what `tests/gate_sparse_hpb.cu`\nnow is. `nvcc`-and-run cost 90 seconds and would have caught this at any point in the intervening\nweeks.\n\n**Second: the mechanism in the comment was the wrong one, and it was wrong in the flattering\ndirection.** Both B9's comment and `DECODE_LADDER` 1.7 attributed the kernel's cost to **reuse**:\n`num_key_value_heads == 1`, so all 64 heads of a query gather the identical KV rows and 63/64ths of\nthe traffic is redundant. It is a correct, checkable, load-bearing-sounding observation. It is also\n**not the binding constraint** — `hpb`, which is precisely the fix for it, measures **1.00x at\nhpb=2 and hpb=4 at every shape**. L1 was already catching the reuse. The actual constraint was\ninstruction issue (32 scalar loads per warp per row on a kernel with 3–6 warps per SM), which no\none had written down anywhere.\n\nWhat makes this a trap rather than an ordinary wrong guess is that **the redundancy arithmetic is\nspectacular and unfalsifiable by inspection**: 168 MB per compressed layer per step, 63/64ths of it\n\"wasted\". A number that large reads as a diagnosis. It was a description. The check that separated\nthem cost one column in a table — sweep the knob that fixes the hypothesised mechanism *alone*,\nbefore building anything, and see whether it moves. Compare §16: the same discipline of testing the\nattribution rather than the story.\n\n**Corollary for gates: a control that never fails has not been shown to be able to.**\n`gate_sparse_hpb`'s one-ulp negative control came back \"0 of 65536 floats differ\" on its fourth\nshape — not because the gate was broken, but because the row it perturbed was outside *that*\nshape's gathered set. A perturbation that the kernel provably cannot see is not a control. Fixed by\nperturbing a sliding-window row, which every shape gathers by construction.\n==== negative ====\n12:## 1. The most expensive lesson: a fake +28 % that passed every gate\n33:## 2. Retired by measurement\n52:## 3. The negatives that were really instrumentation failures\n71:## 4. Conditionally retired — re-open after the draft-head fine-tune\n88:## 4b. Killed inside the winning iteration — the radix top-k's two dead levers (ladder 1.2, 2026-08-20)\n119:## 4c. Adopted, bit-exact, and worth nothing — the `lim <= topk` early-out (ladder 1.3, 2026-08-20)\n154:## 4d. Superseded before it shipped — the tiled `index_score`, and the cost of picking the wrong reference (ladder 1.5, 2026-08-20)\n195:## 4e. The lever that was already applied — `jetson_clocks` (ladder 3.1, 2026-08-20)\n242:## 4f. Two hypotheses killed on the way to localising the prefill race (ladder 1.9, 2026-08-20)\n270:## 4g. Retired on a null before it was built on — `hpb` alone, and the reuse hypothesis (ladder 1.7, 2026-08-20)\n301:## 5. What the negatives taught","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afb9b966-ee00-4592-a8ea-5fde6048a34a","uuid":"2e7605f9-b8cc-43b6-ac68-f5ab401fc1b2","timestamp":"2026-08-20T18:53:28.763Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"116:## 11. Gate the shape range the engine spans\n125:## 12. The engine stops reproducing itself part-way through a long run, so \"bit-identical token ids\" is not a test there\n179:## 13. A gate that passed, reported as a failure, because the probe it rode on kept no records\n200:## 14. A phase that ran nothing, and reported \"finished\"\n239:## 15. Put a leg the change *cannot* touch inside the same sweep\n278:## 16. A fitted slope is only \"cost per 1000 context\" if the mark is linear in context\n313:## 17. A launch that failed, and a `cudaStreamSynchronize` that called it success\n393:## 18. The device opt-in maximum is not the kernel's maximum, and the safety margin that failed a passing gate\n428:## 19. The variance is BETWEEN